  "notes": "Big-hook dance-pop and pop-country crossover with a belted delivery, straight 2&4 against a half-time parent. The drum block follows the entry: \"programmed kick/clap with a stadium-sized reverbed snare on the backbeat; 16th hats and white-noise risers as fills; the country-crossover records swap to a played kit with tambourine.\" So the snare carries a large reverb tail and a hard transient, `hihat.base` is 16th at a high fill density, the fills are `riser` percussion with the snare-roll ladder off, and `tambourine` is in the lane list for the crossover half. The bass is a filtered synth, not an 808 — \"pumping filtered synth sub on 8ths for the dance mode; simple root bass for the crossover mode\" — so no `bass808` block is authored and the bassline is `offbeat_8ths` at a high `followRootsProb`. Harmony is \"4-bar minor loops\" with \"a i—VI—III—VII Eurodance-adjacent colour\", carried at top weight. The melody block encodes the entry's clearest functional claim — \"she writes topline for other people's records, so the hooks are short, high-contrast and repeated immediately\" — as a 1-bar-dominant `phraseBars`, `twoBarCellRepeat` and a leap-weighted interval spread, with \"wide-interval, and built to be shouted\" as the raised `octaveJumpProb`; \"stacked hey-vowel chants\" is the `octave_echo` counter. ⚠ `confidence` is medium and the research says the parameters are \"lane-conventional\": every drum probability, the humanize pair, the harmonic weights, the melody register and `bpm.mode` 114 (the midpoint of the stated 100—128 band) are INFERRED. ⚠ The entry's [LOW-CONF] mark on the belt/range claim is repeated here.",
